      ":question/id": "aa110f6f-e8a3-5f93-ae17-13ecc7c36e75",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{9x+3x{y}^{2}+6+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{(3x+2)(3+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3x+2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n(3+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{3x+2}\n$$",
